fictional place. The phantom had become flesh. When General Drafting later confronted Rand McNally about copying, they were told the hamlet was no longer fictional — it now existed, because someone had used the map as a guide to reality.\nThis accidental loop — a fabrication entering maps, being copied, then inspiring real-world naming — transformed a copyright trick into a philosophical curiosity. When the store eventually closed, the town became fictional again.\nWhat is the general term for fictitious entries deliberately inserted into maps or atlases to detect copyright infringement? And what was the specific name of this New York hamlet that willed itself into existence?", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:00:39Z", "username": "మాन$", "text": "Atos", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]